WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.789
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.789 --> 00:00:07.251
Rose, we're so
excited to have you here today.

00:00:07.251 --> 00:00:08.183
Thank you for coming.

00:00:08.183 --> 00:00:08.789
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:00:08.789 --> 00:00:10.891
It's great to be here at Stanford.

00:00:10.891 --> 00:00:12.071
It's just amazing.

00:00:12.071 --> 00:00:13.090
It's wonderful.

00:00:13.090 --> 00:00:16.113
&gt;&gt; Now, I'm not sure if you know this,
but Patagonia is practically

00:00:16.113 --> 00:00:17.835
the unofficial uniform of GSP-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:00:17.835 --> 00:00:18.989
&gt;&gt; Students.

00:00:18.989 --> 00:00:21.132
&gt;&gt; There's a cheer around
campus a little bit today.

00:00:21.132 --> 00:00:23.688
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; In preparation for

00:00:23.688 --> 00:00:26.862
this interview, I undertook a rigorous

00:00:26.862 --> 00:00:30.438
study to analyze how many GSP wearing-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:00:30.438 --> 00:00:33.605
&gt;&gt; Students I would find in just one day

00:00:33.605 --> 00:00:39.739
on campus, and so without even trying,
I found them In the library.

00:00:39.739 --> 00:00:43.047
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; In the dining hall, in class,

00:00:43.047 --> 00:00:44.648
in town square.

00:00:44.648 --> 00:00:47.578
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And everywhere-

00:00:47.578 --> 00:00:48.385
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:00:48.385 --> 00:00:49.206
&gt;&gt; In between.

00:00:49.206 --> 00:00:52.661
&gt;&gt; That's good [LAUGH].

00:00:52.661 --> 00:00:59.301
&gt;&gt; I even found some GSP students clad
head-to-toe in Patagonia in Patagonia.

00:00:59.301 --> 00:01:04.140
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So, while the GSP students clearly love

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:07.330
Patagonia products, what we love even
more is the company's leadership and

00:01:07.330 --> 00:01:10.640
values, and we're really excited
to explore those with you today.

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:12.830
&gt;&gt; Thank you, it's great to be here.

00:01:12.830 --> 00:01:17.150
&gt;&gt; So, I'd love to start with your
personal journey before you found your way

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:18.052
to Patagonia.

00:01:18.052 --> 00:01:22.486
Early in your career, you reached the
heights of professional success in PE and

00:01:22.486 --> 00:01:23.025
in tech.

00:01:23.025 --> 00:01:23.703
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:01:23.703 --> 00:01:26.846
&gt;&gt; You were the CFO of
a public company and later,

00:01:26.846 --> 00:01:29.135
the EVP of a private equity firm.

00:01:29.135 --> 00:01:30.160
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; And in 2006,

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:32.360
you walked away from it all.

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:33.240
&gt;&gt; I did.

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:34.120
&gt;&gt; Can you share with us why?

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:39.370
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so, I think like anyone,
when you start out your career,

00:01:39.370 --> 00:01:43.484
you're sort of striving to
figure out who you are.

00:01:43.484 --> 00:01:47.532
You're striving to do work
that is meaningful to you,

00:01:47.532 --> 00:01:51.540
even though you don't really
know what that is yet.

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:53.170
You're just figuring it out.

00:01:53.170 --> 00:01:58.020
And I got to this place where my

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:02.515
Italian immigrant family was like,
wow you've made it.

00:02:02.515 --> 00:02:05.393
You're the CFO of a public company.

00:02:05.393 --> 00:02:13.045
I was in my 30s and I was also going
through a spiritual sort of journey.

00:02:13.045 --> 00:02:18.196
I was studying Buddhism at the time and
I got to a place where my

00:02:18.196 --> 00:02:24.458
work life felt completely separated
from my personal life and my values.

00:02:24.458 --> 00:02:29.075
And it seemed to be that way with
every one that I was working around,

00:02:29.075 --> 00:02:33.854
it seemed that there was this
bifurcation between your work life, and

00:02:33.854 --> 00:02:37.013
your personal life and
your personal values.

00:02:37.013 --> 00:02:40.672
And I just couldn't bear that anymore and
then I started working in private

00:02:40.672 --> 00:02:44.870
equity because I thought I'd have more
freedom, it would be more exciting.

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:47.426
And I was really disappointed by it,
and I know some of you are going to go,

00:02:47.426 --> 00:02:50.855
[LAUGH] working price of equity,
so I feel kind of bad saying this.

00:02:50.855 --> 00:02:56.130
[LAUGH] But for me personally,
I could see that the buying and

00:02:56.130 --> 00:02:59.930
selling of companies made
very few people rich, and

00:02:59.930 --> 00:03:04.750
in most cases, in a lot of cases, hurt the
people that were the actual workers, and

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:06.650
the people that had built the company.

00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:10.820
So, yeah,
I went on a sort of personal exploration,

00:03:10.820 --> 00:03:13.720
I went to India, I took some time off.

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:16.950
I took care of my ailing mom at the time.

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:21.373
And I thought about different things that
I could do with my life and I thought,

00:03:21.373 --> 00:03:25.946
well, maybe I should work for an NGO but
then I thought I know nothing about that.

00:03:25.946 --> 00:03:29.173
And I thought maybe I should
become a Buddhist nun and

00:03:29.173 --> 00:03:31.458
then I thought that's not so great.

00:03:31.458 --> 00:03:33.955
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:03:33.955 --> 00:03:38.489
I thought I'd use sort of the skills that
I had, which was making people money,

00:03:38.489 --> 00:03:42.617
being a really good operator,
being a good leader, a good manager, but

00:03:42.617 --> 00:03:46.690
do something with it that was
more aligned with my values.

00:03:46.690 --> 00:03:50.230
&gt;&gt; And I think for many people, this idea
of giving up a path that is safe and

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:53.280
conventionally prestigious
feels very scary.

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:54.920
You actually did it.

00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:55.853
What did you learn from that?

00:03:55.853 --> 00:03:57.573
Was it as scary as people think?

00:03:57.573 --> 00:04:01.752
&gt;&gt; It's funny, the people around me that
were very trapped in their structure were

00:04:01.752 --> 00:04:05.868
calling me and saying, you're doing
the wrong thing, you're gone too long,

00:04:05.868 --> 00:04:08.274
you're leaving in
the height of your career.

00:04:08.274 --> 00:04:12.738
[LAUGH] I mean, it was like, there was
a lot of advice and it was all wrong.

00:04:12.738 --> 00:04:17.472
I mean, [LAUGH] that's basically
what I can say about that.

00:04:17.472 --> 00:04:21.034
I think that once you know that
you want to make a change,

00:04:21.034 --> 00:04:25.460
I think you start to visualize that
you're going to make that change.

00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:27.830
&gt;&gt; And it sounds like during that time,

00:04:27.830 --> 00:04:30.820
you were reflecting a lot
on what matters to you.

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:32.700
What does the outdoors mean to you and

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:35.750
how did that shape your decision
to focus on the environment?

00:04:35.750 --> 00:04:42.210
&gt;&gt; Well, nature, to me,
it's like a spiritual experience.

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:44.790
And it's an experience of awe.

00:04:44.790 --> 00:04:48.800
And we don't get a lot of those
experiences I think in our

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:51.450
day to day life, and
we want to protect it.

00:04:51.450 --> 00:04:56.620
But I think what was more interesting
to me was that Yvon had this model,

00:04:56.620 --> 00:05:00.220
where he was basically saying,
you can have a great business,

00:05:00.220 --> 00:05:03.860
you can make quality product, but
you can also do the right thing

00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:09.040
by the environment,
by your employees, by your community.

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:14.650
And that to me was like the most holistic
vision of business that I had ever seen.

00:05:14.650 --> 00:05:18.832
And I wanted to see if it actually worked,
and how it worked.

00:05:18.832 --> 00:05:22.270
And so that's sort of what
brought me to Patagonia.

00:05:22.270 --> 00:05:25.100
&gt;&gt; Yeah, absolutely, and so you joined
Patagonia, which, as you mentioned,

00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:28.600
is a company with long standing
commitment to environmentalism.

00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:32.280
But under your leadership,
the mission has become even bolder.

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:35.790
Patagonia is now in the business
of saving the home planet.

00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:39.196
Why did you think it was
important to aim even higher?

00:05:39.196 --> 00:05:43.238
&gt;&gt; Yeah, well,
that was really Ivan saying we had so

00:05:43.238 --> 00:05:48.535
fully embodied the old mission
statement that we felt the urgency

00:05:48.535 --> 00:05:53.735
of the climate crisis and
the extinction that we're facing is so

00:05:53.735 --> 00:05:57.682
urgent that we felt like
everybody's mission,

00:05:57.682 --> 00:06:02.719
every business's mission should
be to save our home planet.

00:06:02.719 --> 00:06:07.485
I mean,
we're facing this extinction crisis and

00:06:07.485 --> 00:06:12.251
we can't turn away from it,
and we need the rest of

00:06:12.251 --> 00:06:17.697
business to sort of wake up and
turn towards solutions and

00:06:17.697 --> 00:06:22.926
innovations and
the things that will help us survive.

00:06:22.926 --> 00:06:27.714
&gt;&gt; And Yvon in some ways had this
pioneering, almost anti-corporate vision

00:06:27.714 --> 00:06:31.210
of responsible business,
and since you've joined,

00:06:31.210 --> 00:06:35.780
you've quadrupled revenue to reach
over a billion dollars annually.

00:06:35.780 --> 00:06:38.455
Some people might think that
driving both growth and

00:06:38.455 --> 00:06:41.739
environmental stewardship at
the same time is impossible, but

00:06:41.739 --> 00:06:43.683
you've seemingly done it-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:06:43.683 --> 00:06:44.259
&gt;&gt; How?

00:06:44.259 --> 00:06:48.770
&gt;&gt; I don't think that they're
disconnected in any way.

00:06:48.770 --> 00:06:51.660
I mean,
I think at the foundation of the business,

00:06:51.660 --> 00:06:57.630
it's making a great product and
then standing behind that product.

00:06:57.630 --> 00:07:02.770
So, I don't think that is
in anyway bifurcated from

00:07:02.770 --> 00:07:06.270
caring about our planet and
our environment.

00:07:06.270 --> 00:07:13.580
I mean, the businesses was founded
by people who- Yvon himself made so

00:07:13.580 --> 00:07:16.880
many first descents and
climbs in his life.

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:19.830
I mean,
they're people that love the outdoors and

00:07:19.830 --> 00:07:23.140
saw the changes that were happening
to the outdoors over time.

00:07:23.140 --> 00:07:28.040
because if you keep going back to these
places, you can see that they're starting

00:07:28.040 --> 00:07:32.110
to degrade or they're being
destroyed by the climate crisis.

00:07:32.110 --> 00:07:39.284
So I think Yvon saw that and
it's embedded in the company.

00:07:39.284 --> 00:07:44.848
It's been, I think more important in
the last few years because we have so

00:07:44.848 --> 00:07:48.010
many climate deniers in our government.

00:07:48.010 --> 00:07:51.165
It's been more important for
us to take more of a stand and

00:07:51.165 --> 00:07:53.850
I think our customers understand that.

00:07:53.850 --> 00:07:56.700
And they've been with the brand
a long time and they appreciate that.

00:07:58.280 --> 00:08:01.370
And one of the unique things about
Patagonia is that in many ways,

00:08:01.370 --> 00:08:03.170
it's very counter cultural.

00:08:03.170 --> 00:08:07.670
You donate millions in Black Friday sales
to environmental organizations, and

00:08:07.670 --> 00:08:11.940
you encourage customers to repair and
resell their gear.

00:08:11.940 --> 00:08:13.840
And so as a leader at Patagonia,

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:17.419
how do you decide where to place
these bets on being different?

00:08:19.170 --> 00:08:22.150
&gt;&gt; It comes out of what's
happening in the moment,

00:08:24.020 --> 00:08:28.360
when we donated our Black Friday sales,
it was right after the election.

00:08:28.360 --> 00:08:33.040
We were really bummed out about
what was going to happen to

00:08:34.190 --> 00:08:36.260
the environmental protections that we had.

00:08:36.260 --> 00:08:41.113
We knew that the administration was
trying to get rid of these public lands

00:08:41.113 --> 00:08:43.156
designations that were made.

00:08:43.156 --> 00:08:45.955
And we didn't feel very
festive that holiday.

00:08:45.955 --> 00:08:53.740
[LAUGH] And so we decided that we wanted
to do something that was meaningful.

00:08:53.740 --> 00:08:58.320
And since Black Friday is
such a representation of

00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:03.880
consumerism in a negative way we wanted
it to be turned into something positive.

00:09:03.880 --> 00:09:08.449
And we also wanted to support grassroots
activists that were on the ground and

00:09:08.449 --> 00:09:12.541
needed the money to help fight some
of these environmental battles.

00:09:12.541 --> 00:09:16.190
&gt;&gt; And some of these decisions,
like the one you just talked about, or

00:09:16.190 --> 00:09:17.792
the decision, for example,

00:09:17.792 --> 00:09:22.228
to not sell Patagonia vests to companies
that don't share Patagonia's values.

00:09:22.228 --> 00:09:26.700
Seem like they require some sort of
prioritization between growth and

00:09:26.700 --> 00:09:28.221
values of the company.

00:09:28.221 --> 00:09:32.587
And I think a lot of us are looking to
be leaders that work in that space where

00:09:32.587 --> 00:09:34.284
we're prioritizing both.

00:09:34.284 --> 00:09:37.060
So, how do you actually make
those decisions in practice?

00:09:38.090 --> 00:09:41.030
&gt;&gt; Well, I mean, we keep track.

00:09:41.030 --> 00:09:49.030
We definitely look at what could
be the impact of certain actions.

00:09:49.030 --> 00:09:51.955
But most of the time we're wrong for
the better.

00:09:51.955 --> 00:09:52.950
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:09:52.950 --> 00:09:55.740
&gt;&gt; And that's typically what happens.

00:09:55.740 --> 00:09:56.590
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:09:56.590 --> 00:10:01.520
&gt;&gt; We just decided last year we
gave away the Trump tax cut.

00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:07.270
And this year we had another 10 million
we felt like to give away in addition.

00:10:07.270 --> 00:10:08.320
But we thought this year, okay,

00:10:08.320 --> 00:10:14.160
we're going to ask our customers to
see if they'll participate with us.

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:22.240
And our customers contributed $10 million
to grassroots activists in 17 days.

00:10:22.240 --> 00:10:26.100
And we set this bar thinking $10 million,

00:10:26.100 --> 00:10:29.100
we might not get there,
but our customers did it.

00:10:29.100 --> 00:10:33.670
And because they're as worried
about the environment as we are.

00:10:33.670 --> 00:10:36.020
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think that's what's so
fascinating about the model.

00:10:36.020 --> 00:10:38.170
Is it seems like some of these
moves will hurt profits,

00:10:38.170 --> 00:10:39.914
but they end up having
the opposite effect.

00:10:39.914 --> 00:10:43.040
&gt;&gt; But
these are issues that are our issues.

00:10:43.040 --> 00:10:47.710
They're the issues of our day, and
you can't turn away from them.

00:10:47.710 --> 00:10:50.178
If you're business leaders you
can't turn away from them and

00:10:50.178 --> 00:10:52.480
say, I'm just going to go make a profit.

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:57.360
And forget about the fact that the planet
is burning, or that species are dying.

00:10:57.360 --> 00:11:03.690
Or that there's such gross inequity
between the rich and the poor.

00:11:03.690 --> 00:11:05.960
That it's not going to affect me, right?

00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:12.541
I think if you grow numb in that way,
then we're probably all doomed.

00:11:12.541 --> 00:11:16.447
We need to turn toward it and
actually do something.

00:11:16.447 --> 00:11:21.421
&gt;&gt; Yeah, absolutely, and while we're on
this topic of sort of growth and values,

00:11:21.421 --> 00:11:25.665
at some point, if Patagonia continues
to grow its retail business,

00:11:25.665 --> 00:11:30.591
there could come a tension between that
and sort of the environmental mission.

00:11:30.591 --> 00:11:34.385
And so how are you thinking about
the growth of Patagonia's retail side?

00:11:34.385 --> 00:11:38.736
Is that going to continue to be a focus or
are new business models going to emerge?

00:11:38.736 --> 00:11:39.940
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I mean,

00:11:39.940 --> 00:11:45.521
definitely there's a lot going on
in terms of just resources, right?

00:11:45.521 --> 00:11:49.780
So we're not going to
have unlimited amounts

00:11:49.780 --> 00:11:53.711
of virgin material or virgin resources.

00:11:53.711 --> 00:11:55.996
We've known that for a long time and

00:11:55.996 --> 00:12:00.350
have been working on all kinds of
recycled and reclaimed products.

00:12:00.350 --> 00:12:04.007
Because the reality is,
we're not going to be able to

00:12:04.007 --> 00:12:08.950
keep consuming at the rate we're
consuming and still have a planet.

00:12:08.950 --> 00:12:11.950
So we need to change the business model.

00:12:11.950 --> 00:12:13.470
And that to me is like innovation.

00:12:13.470 --> 00:12:14.600
It's really exciting.

00:12:14.600 --> 00:12:17.830
The people that we have working on that,
they are stoked about it.

00:12:17.830 --> 00:12:24.427
They are blazing new trails and
building new supply chains.

00:12:24.427 --> 00:12:31.440
And so, I think that's just part of
the process of understanding that

00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:35.900
business is changing and
we need different solutions.

00:12:35.900 --> 00:12:38.800
&gt;&gt; Yeah, definitely, and part of
the innovation Patagonia is doing is

00:12:38.800 --> 00:12:42.330
moving into this space of food and
agriculture.

00:12:42.330 --> 00:12:45.610
So could you talk to us a little
bit about how that plays into

00:12:45.610 --> 00:12:46.910
this strategy that you
were just talking about?

00:12:46.910 --> 00:12:50.970
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so we've always been looking at
not just funding grassroots activism but

00:12:50.970 --> 00:12:52.360
looking at solutions.

00:12:52.360 --> 00:12:57.860
Whether it's solutions to supply chain
problems or pollution in the supply chain.

00:12:57.860 --> 00:13:04.872
And in the 90s, our founder had sort of
an epiphany about conventional cotton.

00:13:04.872 --> 00:13:09.831
It uses ten extra pesticides of
most crops, synthetic fertilizers,

00:13:09.831 --> 00:13:13.290
really, really terrible for
the environment.

00:13:13.290 --> 00:13:17.250
And he said if I have
to make another shirt

00:13:17.250 --> 00:13:21.100
with conventional cotton I'd
rather close my business.

00:13:21.100 --> 00:13:25.470
And so he started working on
an organic cotton supply chain.

00:13:25.470 --> 00:13:28.970
So we've always been pioneers in organic.

00:13:28.970 --> 00:13:34.430
And if you look at 99% of the agriculture
in the US is chemical agriculture.

00:13:34.430 --> 00:13:39.050
I'm not going to use the word conventional
anymore because it's not conventional,

00:13:39.050 --> 00:13:40.562
it's chemical.

00:13:40.562 --> 00:13:45.435
And that's 3 or
400 million pounds of pesticides and

00:13:45.435 --> 00:13:49.330
synthetic fertilizers on the soil.

00:13:49.330 --> 00:13:51.792
So if you don't think that's
affecting public health,

00:13:51.792 --> 00:13:57.210
biodiversity loss,
you're kidding yourself.

00:13:57.210 --> 00:14:01.544
So we felt like food was
the best entry way for

00:14:01.544 --> 00:14:06.933
people to really understand
that we can use the soil and

00:14:06.933 --> 00:14:10.703
we can use the Earth as a regenerator.

00:14:10.703 --> 00:14:14.161
And regenerative organic agriculture,

00:14:14.161 --> 00:14:18.904
which is just really techniques,
farming techniques,

00:14:18.904 --> 00:14:23.350
along with organic,
chemical-free agriculture,

00:14:23.350 --> 00:14:28.384
is the sort of solution, I think,
that we need to turn toward

00:14:28.384 --> 00:14:33.549
to get out of this chemical
agriculture model that we're in.

00:14:33.549 --> 00:14:37.254
So there's some estimates that say
we only have 50 or 60 harvests left.

00:14:40.009 --> 00:14:41.675
I know, it bums you out, right?

00:14:41.675 --> 00:14:43.876
But in some way,
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:14:43.876 --> 00:14:47.645
&gt;&gt; In some way it should also excite some

00:14:47.645 --> 00:14:51.180
ideas about wanting to change it.

00:14:51.180 --> 00:14:52.920
And to me, those are the innovators and

00:14:52.920 --> 00:14:57.000
those are the businesses that will really
make a difference, the regenerators.

00:14:57.000 --> 00:14:58.823
We can have a regenerative economy.

00:14:58.823 --> 00:15:01.684
We're going to have to have one.

00:15:01.684 --> 00:15:02.223
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.
&gt;&gt; And

00:15:02.223 --> 00:15:07.760
the businesses that are working on it now
will, I think, be the most successful.

00:15:07.760 --> 00:15:08.723
&gt;&gt; Yeah, absolutely.

00:15:08.723 --> 00:15:12.050
And moving forward, do you see the
agriculture arm being sort of almost as

00:15:12.050 --> 00:15:13.884
big, if not bigger than the retail arm?

00:15:13.884 --> 00:15:15.079
&gt;&gt; I think it could be, yeah.

00:15:15.079 --> 00:15:20.072
And I think we also have set really
strong carbon goals for ourself,

00:15:20.072 --> 00:15:24.810
to be carbon-neutral by 2025,
in our total supply chain.

00:15:24.810 --> 00:15:28.770
And it's really hard to do it,
but we're working on doing it.

00:15:28.770 --> 00:15:33.250
And some of that has to be offset by
sequestering more carbon in the ground,

00:15:33.250 --> 00:15:35.130
through different farming techniques.

00:15:35.130 --> 00:15:36.970
And so we're looking at that as well.

00:15:36.970 --> 00:15:39.890
But yes, to me it's really exciting.

00:15:39.890 --> 00:15:41.490
It's more about innovation.

00:15:42.640 --> 00:15:44.660
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, and

00:15:44.660 --> 00:15:49.770
Patagonia is pioneering all of these
innovative approaches and doing them well.

00:15:49.770 --> 00:15:54.903
Your mission is to save the home planet,
and Patagonia can't achieve that alone.

00:15:54.903 --> 00:15:59.916
And some would ask how replicable is what
Patagonia is doing, given that you're so

00:15:59.916 --> 00:16:03.642
countercultural, long term focused and
privately owned and

00:16:03.642 --> 00:16:08.033
what's your strategy for influencing
others in the broader industry?

00:16:08.033 --> 00:16:13.703
&gt;&gt; Well, I think the benefit corporation
movement has been a really important

00:16:13.703 --> 00:16:19.654
part of showing the business should be
responsible to multiple stakeholders.

00:16:19.654 --> 00:16:24.556
When we became the first California
Benefit Corporation in 2011, and

00:16:24.556 --> 00:16:30.314
now there's I think 50,000 companies that
are taking the B Impact Assessment and

00:16:30.314 --> 00:16:33.457
over 3,000 that actually are certified.

00:16:33.457 --> 00:16:36.323
And so I think that's a nice container.

00:16:36.323 --> 00:16:38.607
That's a nice model.

00:16:38.607 --> 00:16:42.806
I can see the influence now with
the business, Jamie Diamond and

00:16:42.806 --> 00:16:45.374
the business roundtable coming out and

00:16:45.374 --> 00:16:50.761
saying there should be more stakeholders
than just the financial stakeholders.

00:16:50.761 --> 00:16:55.974
It seems to me, obvious there should
be more than one stakeholder.

00:16:55.974 --> 00:16:59.112
[LAUGH] And
then you could balances these things.

00:16:59.112 --> 00:17:03.453
And I think Patagonia does sort of
exemplify balancing those things.

00:17:03.453 --> 00:17:06.753
&gt;&gt; Yeah and one of the things I loved
learning about Patagonia is your

00:17:06.753 --> 00:17:08.797
philosophy on sharing best practices.

00:17:08.797 --> 00:17:13.394
You share openly and you collaborate
even with competitors in some cases.

00:17:13.394 --> 00:17:14.860
Why do you choose to live that way?

00:17:14.860 --> 00:17:18.601
&gt;&gt; Well, it just makes because
we're not a huge company.

00:17:18.601 --> 00:17:22.914
It just helps innovations to scale,
if we do it and

00:17:22.914 --> 00:17:27.022
the North Face does it and
it's it it helps, and

00:17:27.022 --> 00:17:31.659
it's adopted more widely by the Gap or
someone else.

00:17:31.659 --> 00:17:35.737
It makes a difference and so that's
really the philosophy behind sharing.

00:17:35.737 --> 00:17:40.497
And always constantly
being innovative about

00:17:40.497 --> 00:17:45.143
what's next and
what problem to solve next.

00:17:45.143 --> 00:17:50.124
&gt;&gt; Sustainable consumption sadly does
remain financially prohibitive for

00:17:50.124 --> 00:17:55.340
a lot of people and if the goal is to save
the whole planet, how are you thinking

00:17:55.340 --> 00:18:00.012
about reaching the wider audience
is necessary to attain that goal?

00:18:00.012 --> 00:18:06.752
&gt;&gt; Well, I would hope that there's
enough consumer pressure on brands

00:18:06.752 --> 00:18:12.329
that people understand that
if they buy a $2 t-shirt,

00:18:12.329 --> 00:18:18.719
it was probably made with slave labor and
poisonous chemicals and

00:18:18.719 --> 00:18:23.155
they think twice about
buying that product.

00:18:23.155 --> 00:18:28.319
I think that as a brand we've

00:18:28.319 --> 00:18:33.052
always had a belief that,

00:18:33.052 --> 00:18:37.580
we make a great product.

00:18:37.580 --> 00:18:41.380
We'll repair it for you,
if you need it repaired, and it's broken.

00:18:41.380 --> 00:18:45.174
So we're in a contract with
our customer that says,

00:18:45.174 --> 00:18:47.942
we want you to keep that in use longer.

00:18:47.942 --> 00:18:53.277
Because with all the life cycle analysis
stuff we've done over the years,

00:18:53.277 --> 00:18:57.689
the most important thing is to
keep your stuff and use longer.

00:18:57.689 --> 00:19:02.141
And that's the most important
thing a consumer can do.

00:19:02.141 --> 00:19:07.381
And so we'll repair it we'll replace
it if we can't repair it and

00:19:07.381 --> 00:19:12.449
we'll figure out a way to recycle
the materials that you have.

00:19:12.449 --> 00:19:16.398
But you have to the customer has
their part of the bargain too.

00:19:16.398 --> 00:19:19.597
And yeah.

00:19:19.597 --> 00:19:23.981
&gt;&gt; And the work that you're doing on Worn
Wear seems to be an interesting piece of

00:19:23.981 --> 00:19:27.286
this in a sense that it's
attracting younger customers,

00:19:27.286 --> 00:19:31.062
customers who perhaps can't buy
some of the new Patagonia gear but

00:19:31.062 --> 00:19:35.498
they're able to buy some of them-
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I mean there's definitely that,

00:19:35.498 --> 00:19:38.449
we sell we're selling our
US gear we're sort of,

00:19:38.449 --> 00:19:43.162
making sure that it meets the requirements
for being in the secondary market and

00:19:43.162 --> 00:19:45.835
that does help attract
a younger customer but

00:19:45.835 --> 00:19:50.308
it's attracting younger customers to
us now is really more our activism.

00:19:50.308 --> 00:19:54.368
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; And the fact that we're standing up for

00:19:54.368 --> 00:19:57.412
the things that they care about.

00:19:57.412 --> 00:20:01.138
&gt;&gt; And your employees love
working at Patagonia, so

00:20:01.138 --> 00:20:05.136
I wanted to spend a few
minutes on corporate culture.

00:20:05.136 --> 00:20:09.949
It's not hard to see why they love it from
scaling on site childcare to encouraging

00:20:09.949 --> 00:20:12.754
employees to hit the waves
when the stuff is up.

00:20:12.754 --> 00:20:15.643
You invest really heavily in your people.

00:20:15.643 --> 00:20:21.846
Why do you focus so much on this and
what's the return on investment?

00:20:21.846 --> 00:20:25.661
&gt;&gt; Well, let me just say something about
childcare because I feel like that's

00:20:25.661 --> 00:20:28.167
probably the biggest
transformational thing for

00:20:28.167 --> 00:20:31.940
me personally because I worked in tech
before, and I don't have any children.

00:20:31.940 --> 00:20:36.872
So I didn't really relate to
like the kinds of issues that

00:20:36.872 --> 00:20:39.458
my employees might have had.

00:20:39.458 --> 00:20:43.613
There were cappuccino machines,
and Foosball tables, and

00:20:43.613 --> 00:20:45.659
all kinds of cool tech stuff.

00:20:45.659 --> 00:20:50.530
But there was no place that a nursing
mom could go and nurse comfortably.

00:20:50.530 --> 00:20:54.662
And when it came to Patagonia,
I was totally blown away because there was

00:20:54.662 --> 00:20:58.455
on site childcare, there's kids
around our campus all the time,

00:20:58.455 --> 00:21:01.863
which is really great because
you see the future every day.

00:21:01.863 --> 00:21:06.718
And you have a real connection
to your employees children, and

00:21:06.718 --> 00:21:11.035
the next generation, and
that motivates me every day.

00:21:11.035 --> 00:21:13.967
[LAUGH] I mean when I see my employees and

00:21:13.967 --> 00:21:19.843
their children it makes me think about,
what am I doing for the long-term?

00:21:19.843 --> 00:21:25.098
And we subsidize our on-site child care,
but we manage it ourselves.

00:21:25.098 --> 00:21:30.764
And we have the lowest
turnover in our female

00:21:30.764 --> 00:21:35.827
executives, managers, employees.

00:21:35.827 --> 00:21:40.088
We have total gender parity, so
50 50 management women and men,

00:21:40.088 --> 00:21:43.627
that comes from having on-site child care,
I believe.

00:21:43.627 --> 00:21:45.899
Because women don't feel like
they have to take a break-

00:21:45.899 --> 00:21:48.690
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:21:48.690 --> 00:21:50.556
&gt;&gt; From their work.

00:21:50.556 --> 00:21:53.721
And you lose a lot of women after
they have kids because it just,

00:21:53.721 --> 00:21:56.051
it becomes kind of untenable
in lots of ways and

00:21:56.051 --> 00:21:58.822
it's not supported in a lot
of working environments.

00:21:58.822 --> 00:22:03.814
So that's why I think we have
a really strong great gender balance

00:22:03.814 --> 00:22:08.624
company in that way I think it
makes us make better decisions and

00:22:08.624 --> 00:22:13.519
the ROI on it I have done a thing
you can look at, I linked on the ROI

00:22:13.519 --> 00:22:18.416
there is an ROI on it and
yeah I mean I look at big campus and

00:22:18.416 --> 00:22:21.468
like why there is not childcare there.

00:22:21.468 --> 00:22:23.756
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, that was going to
be one of my questions is,

00:22:23.756 --> 00:22:25.460
why aren't more companies doing this?

00:22:25.460 --> 00:22:27.747
And is it really as expensive
as people think when-

00:22:27.747 --> 00:22:30.484
&gt;&gt; No, I don't think I really the economic

00:22:30.484 --> 00:22:34.359
model is on there, but
it's really not that expensive.

00:22:34.359 --> 00:22:35.747
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; I mean, to me,

00:22:35.747 --> 00:22:41.231
it's the benefits far outweigh the costs
and, we subsidize a portion but

00:22:41.231 --> 00:22:45.211
it's not in any way prohibitive or
to our business and

00:22:45.211 --> 00:22:51.252
they've been doing it Patagonia has been
doing it since we were a small company.

00:22:51.252 --> 00:22:53.980
So it's just built into
the business model.

00:22:53.980 --> 00:22:55.309
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:22:55.309 --> 00:22:56.991
&gt;&gt; An incredible inspiration and

00:22:56.991 --> 00:23:01.140
I hope that one that we'll all take
with us as future leaders and implement.

00:23:01.140 --> 00:23:04.376
So if we zoom out from Patagonia's
business and culture and

00:23:04.376 --> 00:23:07.548
talk a bit more about your
views on corporate leadership,

00:23:07.548 --> 00:23:11.970
particularly in the 21st century with
all of the problems that we're facing.

00:23:11.970 --> 00:23:16.554
Under your leadership Patagonia
has become much more political.

00:23:16.554 --> 00:23:19.675
You mentioned earlier you even
sued the Trump administration for

00:23:19.675 --> 00:23:21.440
his plans to roll back public lands.

00:23:21.440 --> 00:23:26.328
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Why

00:23:26.328 --> 00:23:30.213
did you ramp up your political
activism and, in the lead up to 2020,

00:23:30.213 --> 00:23:34.048
is this something that all business
leaders should be thinking about?

00:23:34.048 --> 00:23:36.261
&gt;&gt; Well, I think we've seen a lot, I mean,

00:23:36.261 --> 00:23:39.762
when the administration pulled
out of the Paris Climate Accord.

00:23:39.762 --> 00:23:45.690
We're the only major country not in
the Paris Climate Accord, it's a disgrace.

00:23:45.690 --> 00:23:50.577
And you saw business leaders step up and
sign a document saying we

00:23:50.577 --> 00:23:55.464
are still in on any court or
any team working toward this goals and

00:23:55.464 --> 00:23:59.510
I think those the first time
I was like well this is.

00:23:59.510 --> 00:24:05.200
Waking up and they're starting to
really participate and take a stand.

00:24:05.200 --> 00:24:10.290
And our reaction to what
this administration has done

00:24:10.290 --> 00:24:15.510
is I think proportional, given that
the company has always been about

00:24:15.510 --> 00:24:21.270
protecting wild places and
protecting the environment.

00:24:21.270 --> 00:24:27.410
When Trump signs away 3 million acres
of public land with a stroke of a pen.

00:24:27.410 --> 00:24:29.470
We're not going to sit silent about that.

00:24:30.530 --> 00:24:38.340
And it was a definitely
a difficult choice to make,

00:24:38.340 --> 00:24:43.490
to sue the administration, but it was
the best choice that we had in that time.

00:24:43.490 --> 00:24:48.150
And yeah, we'd do it again in a second.

00:24:48.150 --> 00:24:51.260
And we've sued him over
the Endangered Species Act.

00:24:53.760 --> 00:24:57.510
And they make us briefs on a number of
other issues, and the Clean Water Act.

00:24:57.510 --> 00:25:02.770
And we're not going to sit
by while all the protections

00:25:02.770 --> 00:25:07.720
to wild lands are dismantled,
we're just not going to sit by and

00:25:07.720 --> 00:25:10.975
watch that.

00:25:10.975 --> 00:25:12.860
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; And

00:25:12.860 --> 00:25:16.060
I don't think other companies should
either because a lot of their

00:25:16.060 --> 00:25:20.980
they probably be interested to know
that a lot of their employees find a lot

00:25:20.980 --> 00:25:25.450
of enjoyment in the outdoors and
spending time with their families there.

00:25:25.450 --> 00:25:33.980
And if it's gone because of greedy people,
then we don't get it back.

00:25:35.240 --> 00:25:39.040
So yeah,
I mean it feels proportional to me.

00:25:39.040 --> 00:25:42.280
I mean people say to me all the time,
you're taking the biggest stand,

00:25:42.280 --> 00:25:46.950
but it feels very proportional
to what the company has been

00:25:46.950 --> 00:25:52.720
devoted to over the last 46 years,
and what we're keeping devoted to.

00:25:52.720 --> 00:25:56.360
&gt;&gt; And you're out there taking a stand
often before anyone else is, and

00:25:56.360 --> 00:25:58.750
you mentioned that's not always easy.

00:25:58.750 --> 00:26:01.580
What helps you to actually
act on these convictions, for

00:26:01.580 --> 00:26:04.150
those of us are hoping to
do that one day as well?

00:26:04.150 --> 00:26:11.950
&gt;&gt; Yeah, well I think that Chris Tompkins,
who's on our board,

00:26:11.950 --> 00:26:16.800
when I took the job as CEO, said to me,
you don't get points for holding back.

00:26:18.590 --> 00:26:21.160
And I thought that was great advice.

00:26:21.160 --> 00:26:25.900
And so, I'll always, sort of, had that
in my brain, we shouldn't hold back,

00:26:25.900 --> 00:26:30.890
we should do what we think is right
no matter what the climate is and

00:26:30.890 --> 00:26:32.680
what the situation is.

00:26:32.680 --> 00:26:37.070
And so yeah, I think it's that, and I also
think I work with some amazing people.

00:26:37.070 --> 00:26:41.643
And we all have the same values
in protecting wilderness for

00:26:41.643 --> 00:26:47.260
future generations and we all care
about wild places being around.

00:26:47.260 --> 00:26:51.320
And if they're not we're not
going to have a world to live in.

00:26:53.300 --> 00:26:57.390
&gt;&gt; And on the flip side, you have spoken
out against tech leaders for their

00:26:57.390 --> 00:27:01.110
sort of refusal to take responsibility for
their impact on society.

00:27:01.110 --> 00:27:03.220
Where do you see the sector falling short?

00:27:03.220 --> 00:27:10.387
And how can we as aspiring tech
leaders be better in the future?

00:27:10.387 --> 00:27:17.090
&gt;&gt; Well, think of how much money like
the few, like richest tech guys are.

00:27:17.090 --> 00:27:20.396
I'm not going to name him by
name because you all know them

00:27:20.396 --> 00:27:22.130
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:27:22.130 --> 00:27:23.600
&gt;&gt; What are they doing?.

00:27:23.600 --> 00:27:25.095
Like going to Mars?.

00:27:25.095 --> 00:27:26.300
[LAUGH].

00:27:26.300 --> 00:27:28.328
I mean, how weird is that?.

00:27:28.328 --> 00:27:31.050
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I mean,

00:27:31.050 --> 00:27:35.790
our planet is like,
we are in dire trouble here.

00:27:35.790 --> 00:27:38.730
There's unbelievable income and equity.

00:27:41.450 --> 00:27:47.120
There's a rise of populism and fascism in
the world, and it's very scary right now.

00:27:49.070 --> 00:27:54.630
There's a climate crisis that
is affecting everyone's life and

00:27:54.630 --> 00:27:57.140
causing billions and
billions of dollars in damage.

00:27:58.145 --> 00:28:03.140
And very wealthy people
could do something really

00:28:03.140 --> 00:28:06.260
powerful about it and
I don't see them doing anything.

00:28:07.450 --> 00:28:09.010
I really don't.

00:28:09.010 --> 00:28:10.850
Self driving cars going to Mars.

00:28:12.080 --> 00:28:12.580
I don't get it.

00:28:17.800 --> 00:28:22.400
I think it's the reason why a lot
of wealth shouldn't be concentrated

00:28:22.400 --> 00:28:25.669
into a few people because
that's what happens.

00:28:27.370 --> 00:28:32.870
And yeah, it's not going to make me
very powerful [LAUGH] crowd here but,

00:28:32.870 --> 00:28:35.330
you know,
it's just my point of view about it.

00:28:35.330 --> 00:28:35.870
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:28:35.870 --> 00:28:37.878
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; This is why we like bringing diverse

00:28:37.878 --> 00:28:40.360
speakers on this place so
get different points of view.

00:28:42.450 --> 00:28:46.870
You also have these great thoughts
on the financial system and

00:28:46.870 --> 00:28:50.390
the ways in which it's sort of
structurally destroying our planet,

00:28:50.390 --> 00:28:52.380
particularly this emphasis
on quarterly earnings.

00:28:52.380 --> 00:28:52.960
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:28:52.960 --> 00:28:55.820
&gt;&gt; And so a lot of us are going to be
going back out into the business and

00:28:55.820 --> 00:28:56.850
the finance world.

00:28:56.850 --> 00:28:59.840
What one call to action will you have for
us on that piece?

00:28:59.840 --> 00:29:03.660
&gt;&gt; Well, I do think that this
quarterly earnings thing is insane.

00:29:03.660 --> 00:29:05.530
I was a public company CFO and

00:29:05.530 --> 00:29:10.760
I saw people just sweating out,
one set of earning per share.

00:29:10.760 --> 00:29:13.975
And it's like, think about your life,
nothing important happens in a quarter.

00:29:13.975 --> 00:29:18.780
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It's such a weird system,

00:29:18.780 --> 00:29:23.390
developed by probably just a bunch of
people you wouldn't want to sit and

00:29:23.390 --> 00:29:24.595
have dinner with, honestly.

00:29:24.595 --> 00:29:27.100
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:29:27.100 --> 00:29:29.630
I feel like it's really
destroying the planet.

00:29:29.630 --> 00:29:32.580
Because it's a short term thinking.

00:29:32.580 --> 00:29:36.040
I don't want to think about
what happened in a quarter.

00:29:36.040 --> 00:29:40.700
I want to look into the eyes
of my employee's child, and

00:29:40.700 --> 00:29:43.090
say, what's going to
happen in her lifetime?

00:29:46.480 --> 00:29:52.920
And to me it's like the whole
idea of this short term

00:29:52.920 --> 00:29:59.420
profit that makes a few shareholders
very rich, it's not a good model.

00:29:59.420 --> 00:30:02.490
I mean it doesn't mean
that capitalism is bad.

00:30:02.490 --> 00:30:08.676
I love capitalism, I love the competition,
I love the innovation,

00:30:08.676 --> 00:30:14.983
I love the working together and
collaboration and the best ideas win.

00:30:14.983 --> 00:30:19.863
I love that and that's like the best part
of capitalism and great products and

00:30:19.863 --> 00:30:23.380
great services and
disruption and all of that.

00:30:23.380 --> 00:30:26.290
So, capitalism needs to evolve.

00:30:26.290 --> 00:30:26.955
That's all.

00:30:26.955 --> 00:30:29.710
&gt;&gt; Mm-hm.
&gt;&gt; It's not necessarily a bad system.

00:30:29.710 --> 00:30:31.400
It just needs to evolve.

00:30:31.400 --> 00:30:36.280
And it needs to consider more things
than just a financial shareholder or

00:30:36.280 --> 00:30:38.090
quarterly earnings report.

00:30:38.090 --> 00:30:40.440
&gt;&gt; Yeah, absolutely, and

00:30:40.440 --> 00:30:44.390
Larry Fink of BlackRock just put climate
at the center of his annual letter.

00:30:44.390 --> 00:30:47.210
We've seen a lot of talk about
these things in the past.

00:30:47.210 --> 00:30:50.730
Do you feel like we're finally reaching
a tipping point where action is coming?

00:30:52.560 --> 00:30:53.540
&gt;&gt; I don't know.

00:30:53.540 --> 00:30:55.500
I see a lot of talk.

00:30:55.500 --> 00:30:59.399
And I don't see a lot of action yet
from that group.

00:31:01.050 --> 00:31:05.040
And we don't have time to not have action.

00:31:06.860 --> 00:31:09.520
When I say there's 50 or 60 harvests left.

00:31:09.520 --> 00:31:11.310
There's a lot of scientists
that believe that.

00:31:13.270 --> 00:31:18.422
When we really look at the climate crisis
and how quickly it's come upon us and

00:31:18.422 --> 00:31:23.097
pretty much everyone in this room has
been touched in some way by fire or

00:31:23.097 --> 00:31:26.683
flood or,
has some relative that's been effected.

00:31:26.683 --> 00:31:31.597
And it's only getting worse,
so I don't see

00:31:31.597 --> 00:31:37.028
the action yet,
I don't see the investment yet,

00:31:37.028 --> 00:31:42.240
I still see using the same measures or
metrics.

00:31:42.240 --> 00:31:45.250
But maybe I'm wrong,
maybe I'm not seeing it all,

00:31:45.250 --> 00:31:49.870
but
&gt;&gt; I thought we turn now to some of

00:31:49.870 --> 00:31:54.800
the broader leadership lessons from
your career and trajectory and

00:31:56.660 --> 00:31:59.970
Many of us in this room at
Stanford hope to found companies.

00:31:59.970 --> 00:32:02.280
We're known for
attracting aspiring entrepreneurs.

00:32:02.280 --> 00:32:06.450
But there's this other equally powerful
path of working alongside founders and

00:32:06.450 --> 00:32:08.960
helping to scale their vision and impact,

00:32:08.960 --> 00:32:12.150
which is very much what you've
done alongside Ivan Shinad.

00:32:12.150 --> 00:32:14.550
What's the most important thing that
you're learned from working with him?

00:32:16.622 --> 00:32:18.011
&gt;&gt; Man, there's so many things.

00:32:20.387 --> 00:32:24.171
Well he's completely fearless,
whenever he makes a decision,

00:32:24.171 --> 00:32:28.940
he doesn't care what anyone says or
thinks or, he's just completely fearless.

00:32:28.940 --> 00:32:34.550
He keeps going, and you might get some
noise over here complaints here there but

00:32:34.550 --> 00:32:36.270
you know,
just he doesn't let it bother him.

00:32:36.270 --> 00:32:39.250
He just he keeps he keeps moving forward.

00:32:39.250 --> 00:32:44.280
And he's very self examining.

00:32:46.200 --> 00:32:52.180
It's really part of the company culture
to live an examined life and all of us

00:32:52.180 --> 00:32:57.710
I feel like do that and
we're all curious people and, yeah.

00:32:57.710 --> 00:32:59.550
And I think that and quality.

00:32:59.550 --> 00:33:02.700
He's always is really
really focused on quality.

00:33:02.700 --> 00:33:05.210
And I think he has a right to
always bring it back to that.

00:33:05.210 --> 00:33:08.800
Coz at the end of the day
we're selling a product and

00:33:08.800 --> 00:33:13.830
we want that product to be the highest,
possible quality and

00:33:13.830 --> 00:33:17.230
we want the relationship
with the customer and

00:33:17.230 --> 00:33:21.290
lucky enough to have some of our
Palo Alto store employees here today.

00:33:21.290 --> 00:33:25.690
And it's like they do the really hard
work of like expressing the brand

00:33:25.690 --> 00:33:28.040
through themselves as people.

00:33:28.040 --> 00:33:32.360
And that is always been really important,
you know aspect of the brand.

00:33:33.590 --> 00:33:37.640
&gt;&gt; This idea of living a self-examined
life, I wanted to ask you about this.

00:33:37.640 --> 00:33:42.430
You have a strong set of inner values that
guide you and anchored in sort of your

00:33:42.430 --> 00:33:45.480
Buddhist and meditation practice
that you mentioned earlier.

00:33:45.480 --> 00:33:47.360
What role does this play
in your leadership and

00:33:47.360 --> 00:33:51.410
what advice would you share with us for
cultivating that inner sense of self?

00:33:54.790 --> 00:34:03.220
&gt;&gt; Well, I think it's really good
always to be self-examining and

00:34:03.220 --> 00:34:07.410
to be curious about differences.

00:34:07.410 --> 00:34:11.680
We've become so polarized as
a country in so many ways, and

00:34:11.680 --> 00:34:16.670
yet there isn't a whole lot of curiosity
about why people are different or

00:34:16.670 --> 00:34:18.790
why they might have different views.

00:34:18.790 --> 00:34:23.880
And and I think my practice
helps me to be more conscious of

00:34:23.880 --> 00:34:28.130
the ways in which I fall short myself of,

00:34:28.130 --> 00:34:33.580
being self reflecting and, and
meditation is a nice tool.

00:34:33.580 --> 00:34:37.340
It really does help you deal when
you're in a high stress job.

00:34:37.340 --> 00:34:41.300
It's good to have some tools that work.

00:34:41.300 --> 00:34:46.335
For some people it's certain kinds
of exercising or whatever, but

00:34:46.335 --> 00:34:51.719
for me it's a real tool to manage my
life and to check in on my decisions and

00:34:51.719 --> 00:34:56.843
whether I feel good and right about
the decisions that I'm making for

00:34:56.843 --> 00:34:58.991
the company and for myself.

00:35:00.835 --> 00:35:03.714
&gt;&gt; Many of us hope to make
pivots throughout our career and

00:35:03.714 --> 00:35:08.260
your pivot from private equity to
Patagonia is an incredible example.

00:35:08.260 --> 00:35:12.120
How do you shift your mindset to adapt to
the new industry that you're in without

00:35:12.120 --> 00:35:15.530
losing the unique insights that
you bring from the previous one?

00:35:15.530 --> 00:35:17.499
&gt;&gt; Well,
I think all of your life experience,

00:35:19.180 --> 00:35:21.870
it comes to bear in any thing that you do.

00:35:21.870 --> 00:35:25.840
And that's probably a common theme,
when you do these talks.

00:35:27.320 --> 00:35:28.590
And,.

00:35:28.590 --> 00:35:31.770
Yeah, I mean, I think that's probably
the biggest The biggest thing is it,

00:35:31.770 --> 00:35:33.760
it's all it all gets incorporated.

00:35:33.760 --> 00:35:38.900
If you're a person who lives you
know holistic life and your life is.

00:35:38.900 --> 00:35:42.580
Kind of melded together your values and
your work and your life and

00:35:42.580 --> 00:35:44.230
I think that's the best way to live.

00:35:46.380 --> 00:35:51.070
&gt;&gt; And you have this quote that says
work is where the self meets the world,

00:35:51.070 --> 00:35:54.500
which I loved and
many of us are graduating in a few months.

00:35:54.500 --> 00:35:58.880
So what is the most important thing we
can do to actually find that alignment

00:35:58.880 --> 00:36:00.450
between our work and our value.

00:36:00.450 --> 00:36:04.670
&gt;&gt; Well, I think work is the place where
the self meets the world because it's

00:36:04.670 --> 00:36:10.420
where you kind of are confronted
with all of these sort of tensions

00:36:10.420 --> 00:36:15.160
that you don't have in your family life,
and you have to navigate through them and

00:36:15.160 --> 00:36:19.880
difficult people or difficult business
conditions or whatever it may be.

00:36:19.880 --> 00:36:25.190
And you learn a lot about yourself
if you're a curious person and

00:36:25.190 --> 00:36:30.290
I've learned I learned a lot from
like the bad bosses that I've had.

00:36:30.290 --> 00:36:34.532
I learned more probably from the bad
bosses that I have from the good bosses.

00:36:34.532 --> 00:36:38.730
There are there things like that, that
in retrospect, when you look back on it,

00:36:38.730 --> 00:36:43.875
you realize yeah, I like,
didn't ever want to be like that.

00:36:43.875 --> 00:36:46.950
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Or I want to incorporate this or that,

00:36:46.950 --> 00:36:50.770
yeah I mean, I think, yeah,
that's sort of the way I see it.

00:36:50.770 --> 00:36:54.850
&gt;&gt; Yeah, absolutely, well,
we wanted to leave time, today, for

00:36:54.850 --> 00:36:56.030
questions from the audience.

00:36:56.030 --> 00:36:58.070
&gt;&gt; Cool.
&gt;&gt; So, I'm going to turn it over

00:36:58.070 --> 00:37:00.610
now to some audience members
who have questions for you.

00:37:00.610 --> 00:37:01.143
Great.

00:37:03.080 --> 00:37:05.216
&gt;&gt; Hi, I'm Maya Granite, I'm an MBA 2 and

00:37:05.216 --> 00:37:08.653
I'm also doing the Masters of
Science in Environmental Studies.

00:37:08.653 --> 00:37:10.220
&gt;&gt; Awesome.

00:37:10.220 --> 00:37:12.340
&gt;&gt; So if we want to save our home planet,

00:37:12.340 --> 00:37:14.460
we need to get a heck of
a lot of people involved.

00:37:15.610 --> 00:37:19.680
But the environmental movement is often
associated with a certain demographic,

00:37:19.680 --> 00:37:24.780
often wealthier often white and
frequently women.

00:37:24.780 --> 00:37:27.310
So, why is Patagonia doing?

00:37:27.310 --> 00:37:31.660
And what can we do as future business
leaders to push that movement to

00:37:31.660 --> 00:37:35.970
greater representation and kind of
include more people in the narrative?

00:37:35.970 --> 00:37:38.110
&gt;&gt; Yeah,
I think that's a really good point.

00:37:38.110 --> 00:37:40.040
That's a really good, good question.

00:37:40.040 --> 00:37:42.840
So what we started doing
this a few years ago.

00:37:44.650 --> 00:37:47.840
With our grassroots giving,

00:37:47.840 --> 00:37:52.120
because that seemed like the fastest
way that we could activate it.

00:37:52.120 --> 00:37:54.699
And we started just looking for

00:37:54.699 --> 00:37:59.580
groups that were outside of
our normal outdoor cohort.

00:37:59.580 --> 00:38:04.160
And a lot of these relationships formed
over time just because you've on

00:38:04.160 --> 00:38:07.160
met people along the way and
they were protecting certain places.

00:38:07.160 --> 00:38:10.250
And so that's how the movement grew.

00:38:10.250 --> 00:38:15.080
And so
we took it more into protecting areas that

00:38:15.080 --> 00:38:19.670
maybe aren't the grand outdoor places, but

00:38:19.670 --> 00:38:27.178
it's a cool group of Latina moms in LA
that are doing air quality testing.

00:38:27.178 --> 00:38:31.140
[LAUGH] We wouldn't have even known
about that group before, and so

00:38:31.140 --> 00:38:35.810
we just started bringing in these,
we started having kind of conferences with

00:38:35.810 --> 00:38:40.693
all these different NGOs and, and just
just like making more connections because

00:38:40.693 --> 00:38:43.947
we're all really connected
in the same movement, and

00:38:43.947 --> 00:38:48.110
it's just about like making
the connections stronger and deeper.

00:38:48.110 --> 00:38:53.180
And I think that's the way that we're
looking at it and we're also you know,

00:38:53.180 --> 00:38:57.559
exchanging, sharing learning a lot
from that process as well so.

00:39:00.228 --> 00:39:01.820
&gt;&gt; I really thank you for being here.

00:39:01.820 --> 00:39:04.282
My name is Ellery and
I'm also a second year,

00:39:04.282 --> 00:39:08.480
I'm organizing the climate conference with
Maya so very excited about this topic.

00:39:08.480 --> 00:39:12.380
And given Patagonia's 2025 commitment
to being carbon neutral and

00:39:12.380 --> 00:39:16.850
the complicated history of carbon offsets,
how does Patagonia think about choosing

00:39:16.850 --> 00:39:20.120
office assets that truly
deliver what they promise.

00:39:20.120 --> 00:39:23.580
And then secondarily how can we
collectively hold Patagonia and

00:39:23.580 --> 00:39:27.320
other companies like Microsoft and
Jet Blue to the commitments they make.

00:39:27.320 --> 00:39:31.180
&gt;&gt; Yeah, this is a really good question
because when I first looked at,

00:39:31.180 --> 00:39:34.830
I didn't really know much about offsets so
when we first started looking at offsets

00:39:34.830 --> 00:39:38.890
as a part of our carbon plan,
someone's told me I could spend $100,000

00:39:38.890 --> 00:39:43.250
that could offset all of our, you know,
all of our carbon in the US and

00:39:43.250 --> 00:39:46.230
I was like,
that makes absolutely no sense to me.

00:39:46.230 --> 00:39:49.670
Like just at a practical
level that makes zero sense.

00:39:49.670 --> 00:39:52.130
I don't know where that hundred
thousand dollars is going but

00:39:52.130 --> 00:39:55.350
it doesn't seem to be going for
what it's, you know, really going for.

00:39:55.350 --> 00:39:58.670
So we're really looking for
like absolute reductions.

00:39:59.700 --> 00:40:04.042
By implementing solar grids or
working on energy deals or

00:40:04.042 --> 00:40:08.204
working on converting
factories that are using coal,

00:40:08.204 --> 00:40:13.375
to using renewable energy and
that's the way we're looking at it.

00:40:13.375 --> 00:40:17.355
Offsets, there are some offsets that
are good that are conservation,

00:40:17.355 --> 00:40:21.084
mostly conservation related,
the stuff that we're looking at.

00:40:21.084 --> 00:40:23.663
And I find that to be a little bit,

00:40:23.663 --> 00:40:29.285
to make more sense because conserved
land is a carbon sink and it really,

00:40:29.285 --> 00:40:35.100
if it's not conserved,
it's likely to be disrupted and destroyed.

00:40:35.100 --> 00:40:39.994
But the market itself is,
I think we have to be very careful about

00:40:39.994 --> 00:40:44.995
how people get to that goal and
are they getting to it in a real way?

00:40:44.995 --> 00:40:48.391
So, just keep asking those
deep questions because,

00:40:48.391 --> 00:40:51.565
that's how you hold people
accountable really.

00:40:55.327 --> 00:40:57.120
&gt;&gt; Hi, Rose, thanks for being here.

00:40:57.120 --> 00:41:00.546
I'm Caroline Lay,
a first year MBA students.

00:41:00.546 --> 00:41:04.451
I have a question about kind of the global
ambition of Patagonia because we all

00:41:04.451 --> 00:41:07.348
know Patagonia is in the business
of saving home planet.

00:41:07.348 --> 00:41:11.615
And it's such a global crisis and
imperative, especially for

00:41:11.615 --> 00:41:16.206
the developing countries,
where we're going to see another billion

00:41:16.206 --> 00:41:19.770
middle class entering
the world stage by 2025.

00:41:19.770 --> 00:41:23.220
So what is the vision and ambition for
Patagonia, on the global stage?

00:41:23.220 --> 00:41:26.628
Do you see more aggressive expansion
of your global footprint, and

00:41:26.628 --> 00:41:29.336
how do you balance that with
your integral strategy?

00:41:29.336 --> 00:41:35.245
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so it's not really
about growing our footprint,

00:41:35.245 --> 00:41:41.904
so much as activating our community
in outside of the US region.

00:41:41.904 --> 00:41:47.538
And we've been in Japan for almost 30
years, we've had a business in Europe for

00:41:47.538 --> 00:41:51.474
almost 30 years and
in South America, and Australia.

00:41:51.474 --> 00:41:56.531
And in all those places what we're
trying to do is, first of all give to

00:41:56.531 --> 00:42:01.679
grass roots organizations that
are really doing things on the ground,

00:42:01.679 --> 00:42:05.916
and that creates kind of a community for
us, to work with.

00:42:05.916 --> 00:42:10.026
And it's one of the biggest changes
that I feel like in my tenure as

00:42:10.026 --> 00:42:14.375
CEO I feel really proud of,
is making that mission much more global.

00:42:14.375 --> 00:42:18.307
And, we use a tool that we
introduced a few years ago called

00:42:18.307 --> 00:42:23.535
Patagonia Action Works, which we're
now long launching around the globe.

00:42:23.535 --> 00:42:28.153
And it really is to connect our customers
with our causes, because a lot of

00:42:28.153 --> 00:42:32.270
our customers just want to know,
what can I do, can I donate time?

00:42:32.270 --> 00:42:33.300
Should I donate money?

00:42:33.300 --> 00:42:38.085
What are the right NGOs and so
we're connecting them together and

00:42:38.085 --> 00:42:40.262
it's been really powerful.

00:42:40.262 --> 00:42:46.879
And we also work on policy issues
with the government, wherever we are,

00:42:46.879 --> 00:42:52.860
environmental policy issues and
we stand up as a voice for that.

00:42:52.860 --> 00:42:58.195
And a lot of our industry is also
concerned about these issues,

00:42:58.195 --> 00:43:02.536
so we work as a convener
to kind of make change, so.

00:43:02.536 --> 00:43:03.410
&gt;&gt; Great.
We have time for

00:43:03.410 --> 00:43:04.749
more questions from the audience.

00:43:04.749 --> 00:43:09.308
So, if anyone else would like to ask Rose
a question, please throw up your hand and

00:43:09.308 --> 00:43:10.910
we will get a mic to you, so.

00:43:13.220 --> 00:43:13.750
&gt;&gt; Hi, Rose.

00:43:13.750 --> 00:43:16.510
I'm Isabel Fisher,
second year MBA student here.

00:43:17.750 --> 00:43:21.670
I came to do my MBA because I really
do believe the private sector has a big

00:43:21.670 --> 00:43:25.240
role to play in addressing
challenges that face the world.

00:43:25.240 --> 00:43:28.385
However, I'm also very aware
that there are certain areas and

00:43:28.385 --> 00:43:31.719
certain challenges where the private
sector's role is limited.

00:43:31.719 --> 00:43:33.814
And I'd love to hear you
speak a little about,

00:43:33.814 --> 00:43:37.524
where you see that distinction lying and
where you see a real role for government,

00:43:37.524 --> 00:43:40.568
non-profits or the private sector
&gt;&gt; Yeah, well,

00:43:40.568 --> 00:43:43.772
there's a lot of hybrids going on, right?

00:43:43.772 --> 00:43:47.480
We notice this whenever we have
to build a supply chain that

00:43:47.480 --> 00:43:49.275
hasn't been built before.

00:43:49.275 --> 00:43:52.770
It's like you need,
sometimes you need an NGO,

00:43:52.770 --> 00:43:56.640
sometimes you need a policy
person in government or

00:43:56.640 --> 00:44:00.920
local government and then sometimes
you might need venture fund money.

00:44:00.920 --> 00:44:05.000
So there's a lot of
interesting hybrids going on.

00:44:05.000 --> 00:44:09.699
And I think that's really the only
way we're going to solve these big,

00:44:09.699 --> 00:44:13.522
intractable problems is by
collaborating in that way.

00:44:13.522 --> 00:44:17.893
And it means a lot,
even if you're a small business,

00:44:17.893 --> 00:44:23.333
it means a lot if you let your voice
be known to your legislatures and

00:44:23.333 --> 00:44:27.335
to become active in policy,
essentially, so.

00:44:41.726 --> 00:44:43.381
&gt;&gt; Hi Rose, thank you for being here.

00:44:43.381 --> 00:44:45.670
I'm First year MBA.

00:44:45.670 --> 00:44:49.690
You mentioned a lot of success cases where
Patagonia is able to collaborate with

00:44:49.690 --> 00:44:53.880
other NGOs, with the consumers,
even with governments to join the cause.

00:44:53.880 --> 00:44:58.669
What are the biggest struggles that
Patagonia is facing when you don't succeed

00:44:58.669 --> 00:44:59.462
in doing so?

00:44:59.462 --> 00:45:04.443
&gt;&gt; Well, I think the biggest struggles

00:45:04.443 --> 00:45:08.942
are just the trying to help people

00:45:08.942 --> 00:45:14.093
understand the scope of the problem.

00:45:14.093 --> 00:45:18.731
Because a lot of environmental problems,
you can say,

00:45:18.731 --> 00:45:22.680
well, we're going to deregulate, round up,

00:45:22.680 --> 00:45:28.325
that most people don't think about,
what are the impacts of that?

00:45:28.325 --> 00:45:32.355
It's like, well that might help a farmer
if you do regular round [LAUGH] but

00:45:32.355 --> 00:45:34.210
what does it do to the stream?

00:45:34.210 --> 00:45:36.157
And then what does it
do to the water supply?

00:45:36.157 --> 00:45:38.247
And then what does it do
to the biodiversity and

00:45:38.247 --> 00:45:40.508
then the animals that
are going to drink that water?

00:45:40.508 --> 00:45:43.521
So it's like people have a hard time,
in some ways,

00:45:43.521 --> 00:45:47.359
thinking about the causation of
certain policies, especially.

00:45:47.359 --> 00:45:52.484
I think that's probably,
the most complicated challenge

00:45:52.484 --> 00:45:57.515
is understanding that there
might be a business purpose.

00:45:57.515 --> 00:46:02.825
But there is also a better way to do
things, and trying to always look at that.

00:46:02.825 --> 00:46:07.550
I mean, we we pollute,
we're a company, we pollute, but

00:46:07.550 --> 00:46:10.679
we also understand that's a problem.

00:46:10.679 --> 00:46:15.260
[LAUGH] So we charge ourselves
a 1% tax on our own sales and

00:46:15.260 --> 00:46:21.490
we give to environmental groups,
that will fight for these causes.

00:46:21.490 --> 00:46:23.980
So, I think you constantly have to be,

00:46:23.980 --> 00:46:27.679
business has to keep in mind
that they have a stakeholder,

00:46:27.679 --> 00:46:32.146
which is the planet and that is a really
hard concept for people to get.

00:46:32.146 --> 00:46:33.667
I don't know why,

00:46:33.667 --> 00:46:39.098
because we're losing biodiversity
at an enormous rate right now.

00:46:39.098 --> 00:46:41.452
We're in the middle of a climate crisis.

00:46:41.452 --> 00:46:45.607
I don't know why it is such a hard
concept, but it kind of is.

00:46:45.607 --> 00:46:50.221
I mean, it's interesting, maybe that's
something you guys would solve.

00:46:50.221 --> 00:46:53.254
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Okay, we're taking one last question

00:46:58.304 --> 00:47:00.312
&gt;&gt; Hi, thank you so much.

00:47:00.312 --> 00:47:05.695
It is awesome to have an openly,
queer business leader on a stage today.

00:47:05.695 --> 00:47:08.790
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I'm wondering if you could share

00:47:08.790 --> 00:47:13.489
a little bit about some mentors who
have helped you get to where you are,

00:47:13.489 --> 00:47:18.344
particularly, as somebody who is out
in a space where there are not a lot of

00:47:18.344 --> 00:47:19.695
people who are out?

00:47:19.695 --> 00:47:27.387
&gt;&gt; Well, I wasn't out early in my career
because it was not cool to do that.

00:47:27.387 --> 00:47:28.572
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:47:28.572 --> 00:47:33.816
then when I came to Silicon Valley and
I became CFO, General Magic,

00:47:33.816 --> 00:47:37.779
I was like,
I cannot go into this very public role.

00:47:37.779 --> 00:47:42.219
Because when you're a CFO, you're on all
the road shows and things like that, and

00:47:42.219 --> 00:47:43.054
not be myself.

00:47:43.054 --> 00:47:48.708
I just decided I was drawing a line,
I'm not doing it anymore [LAUGH].

00:47:48.708 --> 00:47:52.838
And when I told the head of HR,
when they were making the offer to me,

00:47:52.838 --> 00:47:54.440
when I told the head of HR.

00:47:54.440 --> 00:47:56.626
She just looked at me and said, my God,

00:47:56.626 --> 00:47:58.943
I thought you were going to
say you had cancer.

00:47:58.943 --> 00:48:02.968
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And [LAUGH] [LAUGH]

00:48:02.968 --> 00:48:04.760
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] And she goes, what do you mean?

00:48:04.760 --> 00:48:06.015
It was Silicon Valley.

00:48:06.015 --> 00:48:09.173
It was a very welcoming community.

00:48:09.173 --> 00:48:14.490
But yeah, I had a boss early in my
career that asked me not to act so

00:48:14.490 --> 00:48:15.977
gay in meetings.

00:48:15.977 --> 00:48:21.440
I mean, I've had weird stuff
like that happen in my career.

00:48:22.580 --> 00:48:26.166
And it's like you just sort of evolve and
you go through it.

00:48:26.166 --> 00:48:30.143
Right now it's just such
a part of me I don't even

00:48:30.143 --> 00:48:34.132
think about it until you
mentioned it actually.

00:48:34.132 --> 00:48:37.580
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:48:37.580 --> 00:48:38.429
&gt;&gt; Great, so,

00:48:38.429 --> 00:48:42.460
we usually end our talks on
a light hearted lightning round.

00:48:42.460 --> 00:48:44.381
And for this one I thought we
could get the audience involved.

00:48:44.381 --> 00:48:44.972
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So,

00:48:44.972 --> 00:48:50.834
we're going to play a quick game of
Patagonia workforce policy trivia.

00:48:50.834 --> 00:48:53.411
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So, I'm going to share a series of

00:48:53.411 --> 00:48:56.670
policies, all of which are true or false.

00:48:56.670 --> 00:48:59.556
And I'd like the audience to just yell out
whether you think they're true or false.

00:48:59.556 --> 00:49:01.792
And then Rose if you could
reveal the right answer.

00:49:01.792 --> 00:49:03.710
&gt;&gt; Okay.
&gt;&gt; That would be great.

00:49:03.710 --> 00:49:04.612
&gt;&gt; I hope I know it.

00:49:04.612 --> 00:49:05.411
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:49:05.411 --> 00:49:08.420
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It's a critical part of the game.

00:49:08.420 --> 00:49:09.970
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Okay, so number one.

00:49:09.970 --> 00:49:12.640
Employees at Patagonia get
every other Friday off.

00:49:12.640 --> 00:49:14.255
True or false?

00:49:14.255 --> 00:49:16.114
&gt;&gt; [CROSSTALK]
&gt;&gt; Ooh, mixed,

00:49:16.114 --> 00:49:18.790
I thought this was the easy one.

00:49:18.790 --> 00:49:20.010
&gt;&gt; True.

00:49:20.010 --> 00:49:21.749
&gt;&gt; True.
&gt;&gt; True at our corporate office,

00:49:21.749 --> 00:49:23.660
not in all of our stores is true.

00:49:23.660 --> 00:49:25.193
We have other ways of doing that.

00:49:25.193 --> 00:49:27.731
&gt;&gt; Patagonia sponsors two
months of paid leave for

00:49:27.731 --> 00:49:31.696
employees to volunteer with
an environmental organization.

00:49:31.696 --> 00:49:32.225
&gt;&gt; True.
&gt;&gt; True.

00:49:32.225 --> 00:49:33.483
&gt;&gt; True.
&gt;&gt; True.

00:49:33.483 --> 00:49:35.359
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:49:35.359 --> 00:49:40.417
&gt;&gt; Patagonia has a buy-back program under

00:49:40.417 --> 00:49:47.863
which it purchases all North Face
clothing from new hires.

00:49:47.863 --> 00:49:49.841
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; False.

00:49:49.841 --> 00:49:50.925
&gt;&gt; False.

00:49:50.925 --> 00:49:53.416
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But maybe a future policy,

00:49:53.416 --> 00:49:55.290
we don't know.

00:49:55.290 --> 00:49:59.512
Patagonia pays for
its nursing mothers to bring that baby and

00:49:59.512 --> 00:50:01.430
a nanny on business trips?

00:50:01.430 --> 00:50:02.128
&gt;&gt; True.
[CROSSTALK]

00:50:02.128 --> 00:50:02.916
&gt;&gt; True.

00:50:02.916 --> 00:50:04.369
&gt;&gt; Amazing.

00:50:04.369 --> 00:50:09.924
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Patagonia subsidizes the installation

00:50:09.924 --> 00:50:15.886
of solar panels in all employees' homes.

00:50:15.886 --> 00:50:17.820
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; False.

00:50:17.820 --> 00:50:19.397
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; False.

00:50:19.397 --> 00:50:22.688
&gt;&gt; False. [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; Okay, two more.

00:50:22.688 --> 00:50:28.304
Patagonia rotates its annual retreats
through the US National Parks?

00:50:28.304 --> 00:50:29.298
[CROSSTALK]
&gt;&gt; True.

00:50:29.298 --> 00:50:30.030
&gt;&gt; Ooh mixed bag.

00:50:30.030 --> 00:50:30.781
&gt;&gt; False.
&gt;&gt; False,

00:50:30.781 --> 00:50:31.296
but-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:50:31.296 --> 00:50:33.522
&gt;&gt; It might not be a bad idea.

00:50:33.522 --> 00:50:34.849
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:50:34.849 --> 00:50:36.716
&gt;&gt; And Patagonia posts bail for

00:50:36.716 --> 00:50:41.626
employees that are arrested for
peacefully protesting the environment.

00:50:41.626 --> 00:50:43.008
&gt;&gt; True [CROSSTALK]
&gt;&gt; True.

00:50:43.008 --> 00:50:45.208
&gt;&gt; True, amazing.
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:50:45.208 --> 00:50:47.528
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Thank you guys.

00:50:47.528 --> 00:50:51.159
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; I think-

00:50:51.159 --> 00:50:54.010
&gt;&gt; Thank you Karen.

00:50:54.010 --> 00:50:56.554
&gt;&gt; At this point we can
all understand why it's so

00:50:56.554 --> 00:50:58.585
